## Finding

The [original incident](/kb/incidents/4471) says that every report is one parser problem.
Its live cause table contradicts that claim. The export contains two different problems;
one queued parser fix does not address both. The counts below describe the 4 October export;
the engineering outcome comes from the later Challenge-page update.

## Engineering outcome · 5 October

The [course's engineering update](https://hq.mega.dev/challenges/what-it-shows) says
`#4471 · closed 05 oct`:

- **Parser — fixed:** `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`. Parser issue 1 is closed
  as completed, based on this engineering evidence, not merely on finishing the investigation.
- **Hint — won't fix:** `booking-v3 form retired`. Form-hint issue 2 is closed as not
  planned, not as fixed. Retiring the form is not evidence that its hint was repaired.

These distinct outcomes replace the original incident's blanket “known, one fix queued”
claim without changing the historical export or its counts. Recheck the live queue before
triaging a new report against either closure.

<Callout kind="warn">
Keep the parser and form-hint problems separate. A shared subject, a browser locale,
or an intake tag does not establish a shared cause.
</Callout>

## Problems and counts

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The table deduplicates by report ID before filtering for the merged ticket. Classification
uses the tenant directory, the platform's locale date order, the tenant's form version,
and the report's channel. Intake tags are guesses, not the classification rule.

### Parser: month-first interpretation for day-first tenants

The shared parser treats a day-first tenant's date as month first. For example,
`R-41203` reports a booking for 2 December whose invite says 12 February.
`R-41200` and its correction `R-41201` illustrate why the wording alone is not reliable.

The scope includes app and email reports as well as booking-v2 form reports; app and email
can still reach this cause for tenants that run booking-v3. Fix date interpretation at the
parser boundary, using the tenant's date format rather than the reporter's browser locale.
Test ambiguous dates, dates whose day exceeds twelve, and month-first tenants as a control.

The cause table preserves the desk's one-day-move caveat. Those reports describe a different
symptom but the supplied classifier files them under parser. Their text alone does not
prove a separate timezone bug; keep the discrepancy visible and investigate before splitting again.

### Hint: booking-v3 instructs month first but reads day first

For day-first tenants on booking-v3, the form prints `MM/DD/YYYY`, but reads the value in
the tenant's day-first order. This is a separate form-hint defect, not fixed merely by
changing the shared parser. Affected tenant handles are `alder.physio`, `copper.studio`,
and `pale.harbour`. Align the displayed hint and the form's interpretation with the tenant
locale, and test the date that reaches the booking and invite.

The corresponding tickets are
[parser issue 1](https://github.com/pawel-kowalczuk-allegro/MEGA_DEV_PUBLIC_S01E03/issues/1)
and [form-hint issue 2](https://github.com/pawel-kowalczuk-allegro/MEGA_DEV_PUBLIC_S01E03/issues/2).
Each includes the computed unique report count and report IDs for audit. The table above
shows the original desk export's ticket ownership, before these new tickets were filed.

## The desk still receives reports

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

Compare received reports with merge activity. A burst of merges records desk processing,
not the start of the defect, and marking the incident known did not stop incoming reports.
Recompute from a fresh export before using these counts for the live queue.

## Triage from here

The [subject-only rule](/kb/triage/dedupe) and
[accepted decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) were last reviewed in 2024.
They are stale and conflict with the executable deduplication examples below.
The accepted decision is preserved unchanged; this page records the contrary evidence,
not an unapproved rewrite of that decision.

<DedupeExplorer />

For each incoming report, resolve its tenant, channel, locale, and form version before
matching a cause. Same subject can hide different causes; different subjects can name the
same cause. An unknown tenant is unresolved, not a match: investigate rather than merge.
Use the cause-specific issue, retain the original report ID, and recompute its count.

